# Patch and diff files must reach the working tree byte-for-byte as committed.
#
# This repository is used with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files to CRLF on checkout. vcpkg applies
# overlay-port patches with `git apply`, and `git apply` REFUSES a CRLF patch against an LF source -- while plain
# `patch` tolerates it, so the difference is easy to miss. The result is a patch that is silently not applied: the
# port builds against unpatched sources and fails somewhere unrelated, or worse, succeeds subtly wrong.
#
# That is not hypothetical here. vcpkg_overlays/ports/gstreamer/gstfilesink_fix_gcc15-2-Rev11.patch was CRLF and
# `git apply --check` rejected it on the 1.28.6 tree; `patch --dry-run` accepted the same file. Marking these paths
# -text takes them out of the conversion machinery entirely, in both directions.
*.patch -text
*.diff  -text

# Shell scripts are checked out with LF, not converted to CRLF like everything else.
#
# They survive CRLF here only because MSYS2's bash tolerates it -- verified: a script taken from a fresh clone, with
# a literal "#!/bin/bash" shebang, still runs. That is a property of the MSYS runtime and not a reason to ship it.
# The same file on a Linux box fails with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M: no such file or directory", and a CR that
# ends up inside a variable turns a string comparison into a puzzle.
#
# Unlike -text above, this normalises in both directions: LF in the repository, LF in the working tree. Nothing here
# is affected today, since every tracked .sh is already LF in the repository.
#
# NOT applied to *.bat. cmd.exe is the one interpreter that has a real claim on CRLF, and its handling of LF around
# labels and multi-line blocks is exactly the kind of thing not worth finding out about.
*.sh text eol=lf
